1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,623
Two people wake up at six in the morning.

2
00:00:04,473 --> 00:00:07,254
Both work hard. Both work long hours.

3
00:00:07,904 --> 00:00:11,732
Ten years later, one of them is still exactly where they started.

4
00:00:12,382 --> 00:00:16,635
The other has built something that pays them, whether they show up or not.

5
00:00:17,285 --> 00:00:20,121
The difference isn't talent. And it isn't luck.

6
00:00:20,971 --> 00:00:22,664
It's what they were working for.

7
00:00:23,314 --> 00:00:27,185
You've probably heard the phrase: rich people don't work for money.

8
00:00:27,835 --> 00:00:31,350
It sounds like something printed on a motivational poster.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,120
But behind it, there's a real financial principle.

10
00:00:35,770 --> 00:00:39,612
And once you understand it, you'll start looking at your paycheck,

11
00:00:39,612 --> 00:00:42,931
your time, and your future in a completely different way.

12
00:00:49,731 --> 00:00:52,074
Let's start with how most people earn money.

13
00:00:52,924 --> 00:00:54,974
You trade your time for a paycheck.

14
00:00:55,824 --> 00:00:58,363
One hour of work. One hour of pay.

15
00:00:59,013 --> 00:01:03,260
Imagine someone earning thirty dollars an hour, working full time.

16
00:01:03,910 --> 00:01:06,822
That's about two thousand and eighty hours a

17
00:01:06,822 --> 00:01:10,595
year, or roughly sixty-two thousand dollars before taxes.

18
00:01:11,245 --> 00:01:14,049
That's honest work. And there's nothing wrong with it.

19
00:01:14,899 --> 00:01:16,235
But there's a ceiling.

20
00:01:16,885 --> 00:01:19,282
There are only twenty-four hours in a day.

21
00:01:19,933 --> 00:01:24,636
You can work more hours. You can ask for a raise. You can get a better job.

22
00:01:25,486 --> 00:01:26,457
And you should.

23
00:01:27,107 --> 00:01:29,928
But your income is still tied to your presence.

24
00:01:30,578 --> 00:01:34,743
If you get sick, if you stop, if you take a break, the money stops too.

25
00:01:35,593 --> 00:01:37,933
Even a raise follows the same rule.

26
00:01:38,583 --> 00:01:41,952
A three percent raise on sixty-two thousand dollars

27
00:01:41,952 --> 00:01:44,660
is less than two thousand dollars a year.

28
00:01:45,310 --> 00:01:49,014
Helpful. But you still have to show up for every single hour of it.

29
00:01:49,864 --> 00:01:51,339
That's the time trap.

30
00:01:51,989 --> 00:01:54,900
Not because your job is bad, but because time

31
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:57,876
is the one resource you can't produce more of.

32
00:02:01,876 --> 00:02:03,117
Here's the shift.

33
00:02:03,767 --> 00:02:08,113
Wealthy people don't stop working. Many of them work harder than ever.

34
00:02:08,763 --> 00:02:11,112
The difference is what they're working for.

35
00:02:11,762 --> 00:02:13,943
They're not just working for a paycheck.

36
00:02:14,793 --> 00:02:16,406
They're working for ownership.

37
00:02:17,056 --> 00:02:20,526
Ownership of assets. Things that can produce value

38
00:02:20,526 --> 00:02:23,787
without requiring their time every single hour.

39
00:02:24,637 --> 00:02:26,065
Shares of companies.

40
00:02:26,915 --> 00:02:29,066
A business that runs with a team.

41
00:02:29,916 --> 00:02:31,861
Property that generates rent.

42
00:02:32,511 --> 00:02:37,561
Products, ideas and intellectual property that can be sold again and again.

43
00:02:38,411 --> 00:02:40,139
And the data reflects this.

44
00:02:40,789 --> 00:02:46,356
According to the Federal Reserve's Survey of Consumer Finances, wealthier households hold

45
00:02:46,356 --> 00:02:50,672
a much larger share of their wealth in stocks and private businesses.

46
00:02:51,322 --> 00:02:54,464
Most families, on the other hand, hold most of their

47
00:02:54,464 --> 00:02:57,968
wealth in their home, their car and their savings account.

48
00:02:58,818 --> 00:03:00,513
Picture two balance sheets.

49
00:03:01,163 --> 00:03:06,305
The first one shows a nice car, a nice phone, and a house with a big mortgage.

50
00:03:06,955 --> 00:03:11,408
The second one shows a modest car, and a growing list of investments.

51
00:03:12,058 --> 00:03:15,311
From the outside, the first person often looks richer.

52
00:03:15,961 --> 00:03:19,485
On paper, the second person is usually building more.

53
00:03:20,335 --> 00:03:22,955
One group mostly owns things they use.

54
00:03:23,605 --> 00:03:26,216
The other mostly owns things that can grow.

55
00:03:30,216 --> 00:03:33,115
In our first episode, we talked about why your

56
00:03:33,115 --> 00:03:36,266
first one hundred thousand dollars is the hardest.

57
00:03:37,116 --> 00:03:39,573
This is the reason it matters so much.

58
00:03:40,223 --> 00:03:44,191
Once you own assets, your money can start doing some of the work.

59
00:03:44,841 --> 00:03:48,276
Think of every dollar you invest as a tiny employee.

60
00:03:48,926 --> 00:03:51,387
It doesn't need a desk. It doesn't take

61
00:03:51,387 --> 00:03:54,353
vacations. It can keep working while you sleep.

62
00:03:55,003 --> 00:03:59,748
Imagine a portfolio of five hundred thousand dollars that hypothetically

63
00:03:59,748 --> 00:04:03,109
pays four percent a year in dividends and interest.

64
00:04:03,959 --> 00:04:06,152
That's twenty thousand dollars a year.

65
00:04:06,802 --> 00:04:09,548
Without adding a single hour to your work week.

66
00:04:10,198 --> 00:04:14,169
Of course, returns are never guaranteed. Dividends can

67
00:04:14,169 --> 00:04:17,258
be cut, markets can fall, and taxes apply.

68
00:04:18,108 --> 00:04:19,480
Now flip the math.

69
00:04:20,130 --> 00:04:25,912
To replace a sixty-two thousand dollar salary with a hypothetical four percent yield,

70
00:04:25,912 --> 00:04:30,401
you would need about one point five six million dollars in assets.

71
00:04:31,251 --> 00:04:33,171
That number sounds enormous.

72
00:04:34,021 --> 00:04:35,002
And it is.

73
00:04:35,652 --> 00:04:39,649
But it shows something important: every asset you own replaces a

74
00:04:39,649 --> 00:04:43,271
small piece of the hours you would otherwise have to work.

75
00:04:43,921 --> 00:04:48,131
You don't need to replace your entire salary to feel the difference.

76
00:04:48,781 --> 00:04:51,526
Even a few hundred dollars a month in asset

77
00:04:51,526 --> 00:04:54,143
income can give you more room to breathe.

78
00:04:54,993 --> 00:04:56,938
But the principle is powerful.

79
00:04:57,788 --> 00:04:59,845
Your job pays you for your time.

80
00:05:00,495 --> 00:05:03,026
Your assets can pay you for your ownership.

81
00:05:07,026 --> 00:05:10,736
There's another reason the wealthy think differently about work.

82
00:05:11,586 --> 00:05:12,907
It's called leverage.

83
00:05:13,557 --> 00:05:17,646
When you work an hourly job, one hour of effort is sold once.

84
00:05:18,496 --> 00:05:20,809
But some work can be sold many times.

85
00:05:21,459 --> 00:05:25,081
A piece of software. A book. An online course. A video.

86
00:05:25,731 --> 00:05:30,533
You build it once, and it can serve one person or one hundred thousand people.

87
00:05:31,183 --> 00:05:34,890
Imagine a digital guide that sells for fifty dollars.

88
00:05:35,540 --> 00:05:39,339
Sold to one thousand people, that's fifty thousand dollars

89
00:05:39,339 --> 00:05:41,893
in sales, from work that was done once.

90
00:05:42,543 --> 00:05:47,059
Not every product works. Most need marketing, updates and support.

91
00:05:47,709 --> 00:05:51,320
But the structure is completely different from an hourly job.

92
00:05:51,970 --> 00:05:55,083
Leverage also comes from people and capital.

93
00:05:55,733 --> 00:06:00,851
A business owner with ten employees isn't limited to their own eight hours a day.

94
00:06:01,501 --> 00:06:04,546
An investor isn't limited to their own salary.

95
00:06:05,196 --> 00:06:08,156
This is why two people can work the same number

96
00:06:08,156 --> 00:06:11,368
of hours and end up in completely different places.

97
00:06:12,218 --> 00:06:13,824
One sells their time.

98
00:06:14,474 --> 00:06:17,080
The other builds something that multiplies it.

99
00:06:21,080 --> 00:06:24,204
So does this mean you should quit your job tomorrow?

100
00:06:25,054 --> 00:06:25,717
No.

101
00:06:26,367 --> 00:06:30,812
For most people, a paycheck is the engine that makes ownership possible.

102
00:06:31,662 --> 00:06:33,815
Here's a more realistic approach.

103
00:06:34,465 --> 00:06:37,812
First: use your income to buy ownership every month.

104
00:06:38,462 --> 00:06:42,178
Even small amounts. A diversified investment, bought

105
00:06:42,178 --> 00:06:44,894
consistently, is a piece of ownership.

106
00:06:45,544 --> 00:06:50,899
Three hundred dollars a month, invested for twenty years at a hypothetical seven

107
00:06:50,899 --> 00:06:56,588
percent average return, could grow to roughly one hundred fifty-six thousand dollars.

108
00:06:57,238 --> 00:07:02,037
You would have contributed seventy-two thousand. The rest would come from growth.

109
00:07:02,687 --> 00:07:06,966
Not guaranteed. But that's what ownership can look like over time.

110
00:07:07,616 --> 00:07:10,118
Second: build one asset with your skills.

111
00:07:10,768 --> 00:07:14,037
It could be a small business, a digital product, or

112
00:07:14,037 --> 00:07:16,985
a service that can grow beyond your own hours.

113
00:07:17,635 --> 00:07:21,070
Start small. Learn how it works. Make it sustainable.

114
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:24,597
Third: keep increasing the value of your time.

115
00:07:25,247 --> 00:07:28,694
Better skills can mean a higher income. And a higher

116
00:07:28,694 --> 00:07:31,942
income means more money you can turn into assets.

117
00:07:32,592 --> 00:07:37,584
And be careful with anyone who promises fast and guaranteed passive income.

118
00:07:38,434 --> 00:07:40,790
Real ownership takes time to build.

119
00:07:44,790 --> 00:07:47,379
Most people ask one question about money:

120
00:07:48,229 --> 00:07:50,167
How much do I make per hour?

121
00:07:50,817 --> 00:07:53,425
Wealthy people learn to ask a different one:

122
00:07:54,075 --> 00:07:56,735
What do I own that works, even when I don't?

123
00:07:57,385 --> 00:08:01,774
You don't need to answer that with a mansion or a company worth millions.

124
00:08:02,424 --> 00:08:05,411
Your answer can start with your first share, your

125
00:08:05,411 --> 00:08:09,068
first small product, or your first hundred dollars invested.

126
00:08:09,918 --> 00:08:11,969
The goal isn't to stop working.

127
00:08:12,619 --> 00:08:15,739
The goal is to stop depending only on your time.

128
00:08:16,589 --> 00:08:17,961
So let me ask you:

129
00:08:18,811 --> 00:08:21,085
What's the first asset you want to own?

130
00:08:21,935 --> 00:08:23,686
Let me know in the comments.

131
00:08:24,336 --> 00:08:27,159
And subscribe to The Wealth Blueprint for more

132
00:08:27,159 --> 00:08:30,473
stories, strategies and lessons about building wealth.

133
00:08:31,323 --> 00:08:33,518
Understand money. Build wealth.
